 { One of the most valuable features of open files is the ability to use them as routes of invasion into the enemy position. In this section we'll take a look at the fight for various typical entry squares, beginning with the most popular entry target: the seventh rank. In the introductory example we saw how Capablanca activated both rooks along an open file in the centre. However, a single rook will often be enough to cause irreparable damage to the position. This position is from one of my online games, where I had the Black pieces and am in huge trouble with my knight on c7 under attack. } 1... { -KEY- } Rac8 { Defending the knight was necessary, as moving it would have lost the bishop on b7. } 2. Bh3 $1 { A really clever move, and a technique worth keeping in mind in your own games. } f5 { The point of White's last move! Black is forced to further weaken their second rank. } 3. Bh6 $1 { Rg7+ ideas now become possible. } Re8 4. Rae1 $1 { White has no intention of giving up either file or rank. } Rxe7 5. Rxe7 Ba6 { Stepping the bishop out of the line of fire in order to be able to move the paralysed knight. } 6. g4 $1 { The pawn break will allow the light-squared bishop to enter the action. } Nd5 7. Rg7+ Kh8 8. Ra7 $1 { Hunting the stranded bishop. } Nc7 9. gxf5 gxf5 10. Kh1 $18 { White is ready to play Bxf5 next, with his last move designed to cut out the reply of ...Rg8+. Black's position is resignable, as all his pieces are paralysed. Here the problems began when White established their rook on the seventh rank. Unable to challenge the invader, Black's position quickly collapsed. } 
