 { In our previous example, White had already established the rook on the seventh rank. Most of the time, achieving this goal will not be so easy as the opponent tries everything to stop it. When you are in control of an open file, it pays to check for tactical possibilities to land a rook on the seventh rank - even when at first glance it may seem impossible. In the following position, White does exactly this. Let's take a look. } 1. { -KEY- } Bh5 $1 { Offering a bishop exchange. If White can push that through, then the invasion along the h-file will prove deadly. } Bh7 $6 ( { A move like } 1... Qe8 { was a more stubborn defense, but even there after } 2. g4 $1 { the Black kingside will eventually collapse. } ) 
2. f4 { Improving the structure and locking down the advance of Black's e6 pawn. } Bf6 3. Bf7 $3 { Out of the blue, the bishop plants itself on the f7 square. Black's king is overloaded because of the bishop on h7, while the e6 pawn is extremely weak. What's more, White has nasty threats like Qh5 or Rxh7+ next. } Kxf7 ( { If Black ignores the threats with a move like } 3... a5 { then } 4. Qh5 { is perhaps simplest, but the exchange sacrifice also works: } 4. Rxh7 Kxh7 5. Qh5 Kg7 6. Qg6 Kf8 7. Qxf6 $18 { White is up material, and has a deadly attack to boot. } ) 
4. Rxh7+ { White finally achieves the goal of breaking in along the seventh rank. And the Black king is so naked that it's immediately game over. } Kg8 5. Qh5 { The immediate threat is Qf7#. } Bg7 6. Qg6 Qd7 7. Nxe6 { Simplifying the position. } Rxe6 8. Rxg7+ Qxg7 9. Qxe6+ { with a winning endgame, since either Black allows a queen exchange with ...Qf7 or White will win several more pawns. The examples we've seen so far have been quite tactically flashy. Let's close out our seventh rank examples with a not-so-glamorous but very typical situation that you almost certainly have encountered in your own games. } 
