 { Entry into the seventh rank can be so valuable that sometimes it's even worth sacrificing material to achieve the goal. In the 2018 Olympiad I missed a winning sequence from this position in my game against FM Ben Hague. } 21. { -KEY- } Bc5 $1 { A surprising blow, inviting Black to grab two minor pieces for a rook. } ( 21. Nf3 $6 { is what I played in the game, losing a large chunk of my advantage. } ) 
Qxg5 ( 21... Qc7 22. Rac1 $1 { Insisting on sacrificing material. } hxg5 23. Bxf8 Qxc2 24. Rxc2 Kxf8 25. Rc7 $18 { The seventh rank entry proves decisive as Black cannot save the bishop on a7. } ) 
22. Bxf8 Kxf8 23. Qc7 $1 { White enters along the seventh, in this case with the queen. } Qe7 24. Rd8+ $1 { And now the rook joins the party along the eight rank! } Kf7 25. Rc1 { It's time to defend the queen. Incredibly, the game is already over since all of Black's pieces are permanently tied down. Let's show how the game might finish: } e5 26. Bf3 g6 27. Rh8 Qxc7 28. Rxc7+ Bd7 29. Rxb8 Bxb8 30. Rb7 $18 { Black will lose a piece on the next move, and with it the game. It was not only the loss of control over the 7th rank that stung Black, but also the move 24.Rd8+ with a second invasion along Black's first rank. In general, entries from the 6th-8th rank can cause a lot of damage so we should be alert to all of these possibilities. We'll turn to some examples of this next. } 
 *

