 { In the position in front of us, both the c- and d-files are open. However, the d-file is very congested. For this reason, both players turn to a fight for the c-file. } 1. { -KEY- } Rc1 $1 { Even though Black was the first to arrive, it is White who has the upper hand in the battle for the file. The problem for Black is that without access to c8 Black is unable to supply reinforcements to the lone rook on c7. } Rb8 2. Rxc7 $1 Nxc7 3. Rc1 { White lays full claim to the c-file. } Ncd5 4. Bxd5 $1 { A clever move, clearing the path for a future entry along the seventh rank. } Nxd5 ( 4... exd5 5. Rc7 $16 { is really difficult for Black, since now they've got to worry about an isolated pawn on d5 in addition to White having secured the seventh rank. } ) 
5. e4 $1 { This is White's idea, forcing Black to lose their grip on c7. } Nb6 6. Rc7 $1 { White has achieved their goal, and Black's position is already objectively lost. } Be8 7. f4 $1 ( 7. Ra7 { Seeking to immediately convert the positional advantage into an extra pawn would also be enough, though the text move is even stronger as it stops any piece exchange after 7...Nc8. } ) 
Kf8 8. Kf2 Rd8 9. e5 $18 { Black is completely paralysed. White's plan from here will be to play g2-g3, then march the king to d4 (without getting forked!) and eventually find a breakthrough with either a piece exchange or a rook move. Black's problems in this position stemmed from the lack of access to the entry square for their rook. Entry squares are equally important in both attack and defense. } 
