 { In the online game Carlsen,M - Hakobyan,A (2023) , White had to be careful which way to recapture on b3. What do you think is best? } 1. { -KEY- } axb3 $1 { Correct! It's essential for White to stop access to the d3 entry square for Black. } ( 1. cxb3 $2 { would give away all of White's advantage after } Rd3 $1 { The rook jumps at the chance to enter the White position. And from here it proves so problematic for White that there is nothing to do but force a draw: } 2. Nc1 Rc3 3. Ne2 Rd3 ( 3. axb3 Rc2 { White is fine against this move, with either } 4. Rc1 { or } 4. Nc1 { holding the balance. } ) 
4. Nc1 $11 { with a draw by repetition. } ) 
Rd8 2. Nc1 $1 { By coincidence, we will see the Ne2-c1 maneuver in two different games! } Nf8 3. Nd3 { From d3, the knight targets the weak e5 pawn and the vulnerable queenside dark squares. } Nd7 4. b4 $1 { Locking down the structure, turning Black's a- and c-pawns into backward pawns. } Ra8 { Looking to exchange off the backward pawn. } 5. Bd2 $1 Bd6 6. g5 $18 { Black has no good reply against the kingside pressure, while any counterplay on the queenside is far too slow. For example: } hxg5 7. hxg5 c5 8. bxc5 Nxc5 9. g6+ Kg8 10. Nxc5 Bxc5 11. Bc3 Bd4 12. Bxd4 exd4 13. e5 $18 { White's pawns are much faster than Black's. Although White was completely in control of the position, a lapse of focus and giving up the d3 entry square at the beginning would have been enough to ruin all of his advantage. Chess can be an unforgiving game! } 
