 { Sometimes the only path to guarding your advantage along a file will be through the use of tactics. In this position, Black intends to play ...Kd7 followed by ...Rc8, robbing White of control of the open file. There is only one way to put a stop to this plan.Can you find White's strong idea here? (Warning: it's not an easy one!) } 1. { -KEY- } Nc1 $3 { This tricky backward knight move stops Black's plan in its tracks. } ( 1. Qc1 { A passive move would immediately lose all of the advantage. } Kd7 $1 2. Rc2 Rc8 $11 { With the c8 entry square under control, Black is now able to contest the file and has comfortable equality. } ) 
Kd7 $2 { Failing to spot White's devilish idea. } ( { Black should have switched gears and tried to bring the king into the game via d8 , but after } 1... Be7 2. Rc7 $16 { White keeps heavy pressure on. } ) 
2. Nd3 $1 { Correct! Well done if you found this sneaky idea. } Ke8 { Black has no choice but to retreat, but now White can win in a few ways. The cleanest is the kingside pawn break. } ( 2... Qxc6 $2 3. Ne5 { will soon lead to mate. } ) 
 ( 2... f6 $2 3. Nc5 Kxc6 4. Nxb7 Kxb7 5. Qc5 $18 { The queen overwhelms the rook and bishop in this position, mainly because of how weak Black's pawns are. } ) 
3. g4 $1 { Black has no way to challenge the activity of the White pieces, and once things get cracked open on the kingside it'll be game over. For instance: } fxg4 4. Ne5 gxh3 5. Qxh7 $18 { Black can't stop all of White's threats, like Qg8+ or Rxe6+. A lot of the examples in this section have involved quite nice tactical ideas. Let's close things out with an example that's pretty mundane, almost boring, but that crops up quite often in chess so it's one that pays to watch out for. } 
