 { The final position of the section is from an online game of mine. It's a typical situation, where Black has better placed pieces and a safer king. Additionally, the pawn on c4 can be captured. The question to answer, whenever you find yourself in this spot, is whether you should 'cash in' your positional advantage in exchange for some material gains. } 1... { -KEY- } Bxc4 $6 { The greedy capture loses all of the advantage. The problem is that in the series of exchanges that follow, Black loses control over key squares along the open files. } ( 1... a5 $15 { I should have kept the tension, since White's king safety and piece placement were poorer than mine. Here, I would have had some good chances to press for the win. } ) 
2. Qxc4 Qxc4 3. Nxc4 Rxc4 { In my calculations, I had hoped that the control over the 7th rank entry square would be enough. } 4. Rac1 $1 { Unfortunately, I overlooked that White can break in along the c-file instead! } Rxc1 ( 4... Rcc8 { Retreating will not allow me to keep control of the file, for example: } 5. Bh3 $1 { White controls the entry square along the open file, a topic that we'll explore more in the second section of the chapter. } Rb8 6. Rc7 $11 { with a position not too different from what we cover in the main line. } ) 
 ( 4... Rfc8 $4 { If only I had this move, then I'd be able to escape a pawn to the good. However, this would be a losing mistake. } 5. Rxc4 Rxc4 { I would get mated after } 6. Rd8 Ne8 7. Rxe8 { . } ) 
5. Rxc1 Rd8 ( 5... Rb8 $6 { is too passive. Simplest is perhaps } 6. Bh3 { when Black cannot follow through on the plan of bringing the king to the centre since after } Kf8 $6 7. Rc8 $1 Rxc8 8. Bxc8 $14 { if either side is better, it's White. } ) 
6. Rc7 { With the seventh rank secured, the piece activity is too much for Black to be able to claim an advantage in spite of the extra pawn. Play might continue: } b5 7. Ke2 $1 { A clever move, preventing an invasion for Black along the seventh. } Kf8 8. Rc6 Ra8 9. Bh3 { There are many ways to draw as White, but this one seems the most intuitive to me. } Ne8 10. Bc8 a5 11. Bd7 Ra7 12. Bxe8 Kxe8 13. Rc5 $11 { White finally recovers their pawn. The problem was that in the starting position, I misjudged the cost of allowing White to enter along the 7th rank. Once this happened, any advantage had already evaporated. In this section, we discussed several typical entry points, with a focus on the seventh rank. Next up, we'll speak about the connection between open files and outposts. See you there! } 
 *
